Q: Long breadcrumb should be right-aligned when scrolled A: <p>You can use a translation combined with container query units. As a bonus you can have a nice effect on hover.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.container {
  font-family: arial;
  border: 2px solid;
  padding: 20px;
  height: 300px;
  width: 320px;
  max-width: 500px;
  resize: horizontal;
  overflow: auto;
}
.wrapper {
  container-type: inline-size; /* an extra wrapper for the container */
  overflow: hidden; /* hide the overflow */
}
.wrapper:hover {
  overflow: auto; /* show scrollbar on hover */
}
.breadcrumb-list {
  display: inline-flex;
  list-style-type: none;
  gap: 1em;
  padding: .5em;
  margin: 0;
  min-width: 100%;
  translate: min(0px,100cqw - 100%); /* translate using cqw unit to show the end of the content */
  transition: .5s;

  .wrapper:hover &amp; {
    translate: 0px; /* translate back on hover */
  }
  li {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  li:not(:last-child):after {
    content:' |';
    margin-left: .5em;
  }
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div class="container"&gt;
  &lt;h3&gt;Normal: left aligned&lt;/h3&gt;  
  &lt;ul class="breadcrumb-list"&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#"&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;My Account&lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;/ul&gt;
  &lt;h3&gt;If scrolling is necessary, breadcrumb should be right aligned, so article-name is in viewport&lt;/h3&gt;
  &lt;div class="wrapper"&gt;
  &lt;ul class="breadcrumb-list"&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#"&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#"&gt;Men&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#"&gt;Clothes&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#"&gt;Sweatshirts&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;Adidas Hoodie XL&lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;/ul&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: How to separate web components to individual files and load them? A: <p>TL;DR</p>
<p>Follow the patterns demonstrated at <a href="https://github.com/morganney/web-component-best-practices/tree/main/example" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/morganney/web-component-best-practices/tree/main/example</a>. There is a running demo at <a href="https://morganney.github.io/web-component-best-practices/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://morganney.github.io/web-component-best-practices/</a></p>
<p>A generic pattern using <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Guide/Modules#top_level_await" rel="nofollow noreferrer">top level await</a> without side-effects:</p>
<pre><code>my-component/
  element.js
  template.html
  styles.css
</code></pre>
<p><strong>template.html</strong> (be sure to link to styles.css)</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;template&gt;
  &lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; href=&quot;./styles.css&quot; /&gt;

  &lt;!-- other HTML/Slots/Etc. --&gt;
  &lt;slot&gt;&lt;/slot&gt;
&lt;/template&gt;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>styles.css</strong> (regular CSS file)</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>:host {
  border: 1px solid red;
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>element.js</strong> (uses top level await in export)</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const setup = async () =&gt; {
  const parser = new DOMParser()
  const resp = await fetch('./template.html')
  const html = await resp.text()
  const template = parser.parseFromString(html, 'text/html').querySelector('template')

  return class MyComponent extends HTMLElement {
    constructor() {
      super()

      this.attachShadow({ mode: 'open'}).appendChild(template.content.cloneNode(true))
    }

    // Rest of element implementation...
  }
}

export default await setup()
</code></pre>
<p><strong>index.html</strong> (loading and defining the element)</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;!doctype html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
  &lt;head&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;Custom Element Separate Files&lt;/title&gt;
    &lt;script type=&quot;module&quot;&gt;
      import MyComponent from './element.js'

      if (!customElements.get('my-component')) {
        customElements.define('my-component', MyComponent)
      }
    &lt;/script&gt;
  &lt;/head&gt;
  &lt;body&gt;
    &lt;my-component&gt;hello world&lt;/my-component&gt;
  &lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>You can and should make side-effects (like registering a custom element in the global scope) explicit. Aside from creating some <code>init</code> function to call on your element, you can also provide a distinct import path, for example:</p>
<p><strong>defined.js</strong> (sibling to element.js)</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import MyComponent from './element.js'

const define = async () =&gt; {
  let ctor = null

  customElements.define('my-component', MyComponent)
  ctor = await customElements.whenDefined('my-component')

  return ctor
}

export default await define()
</code></pre>
<p><strong>index.html</strong> (side-effect made explicit via import path)</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;!doctype html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
  &lt;head&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;Custom Element Separate Files&lt;/title&gt;
    &lt;script type=&quot;module&quot; src=&quot;./defined.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
  &lt;/head&gt;
  &lt;body&gt;
    &lt;my-component&gt;hello world&lt;/my-component&gt;
  &lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>This approach can also support arbitrary names when defining the custom element by including something like this inside <code>define</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>new URL(import.meta.url).searchParams.get('name')
</code></pre>
<p>and then passing the <code>name</code> query param in the import specifier:</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script type=&quot;module&quot; src=&quot;./defined.js?name=custom-name&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;custom-name&gt;hello&lt;/custom-name&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Here's an example snippet using <a href="https://github.com/morganney/tts-element" rel="nofollow noreferrer">tts-element</a> that combines all three approaches (see the network tab in dev console):</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html lang=&quot;en-US&quot;&gt;
  &lt;head&gt;
    &lt;meta charset=&quot;utf-8&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;tts-element combined example&lt;/title&gt;
    &lt;style&gt;
      text-to-speech:not(:defined), my-tts:not(:defined), speech-synth:not(:defined) {
        display: none;
      }
    &lt;/style&gt;
    &lt;script type=&quot;module&quot; src=&quot;https://unpkg.com/tts-element/dist/text-to-speech/defined.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
    &lt;script type=&quot;module&quot; src=&quot;https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/tts-element@0.0.3-beta/dist/text-to-speech/defined.js?name=my-tts&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
    &lt;script type=&quot;module&quot;&gt;
      import ctor from 'https://unpkg.com/tts-element/dist/text-to-speech/element.js'

      customElements.define('speech-synth', ctor)
    &lt;/script&gt;
  &lt;/head&gt;
  &lt;body&gt;
    &lt;text-to-speech&gt;Your run-of-the-mill text-to-speech example.&lt;/text-to-speech&gt;
    &lt;my-tts&gt;Example using the &quot;name&quot; query parameter.&lt;/my-tts&gt;
    &lt;speech-synth&gt;Example using element.js.&lt;/speech-synth&gt;
  &lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: R Shiny + plotly legend in sidebar? A: <p>I had the same issue, only I wanted to put the legend within a sticky container above a lengthy gantt-chart, so that I can toggle traces without scrolling back to the top. The idea is to just create your own legend within any container really and place buttons inside that show or hide traces via the plotly API. This works really well. Plotly legends can isolate traces on double-click, this part I leave for you to implement :).</p>
<pre><code>library(plotly)
library(shiny)

data &lt;- ChickWeight
ag_data &lt;- aggregate(data$weight,
                     list(time = data$Time, diet = data$Diet),
                     mean)

ui &lt;- fluidPage(
  tags$style(HTML(&quot;
    .legend-btn {
      display: inline-flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 8px;
      margin: 4px;
      padding: 6px 12px;
      border: 2px solid;
      border-radius: 5px;
      background: white;
      cursor: pointer;
    }
    .legend-btn.dimmed {
      opacity: 0.35;
    }
  &quot;)),
  sidebarPanel(div(id = &quot;legend_container&quot;)),
  mainPanel(plotlyOutput(&quot;chick_weight&quot;))
)

server &lt;- function(input, output) {
  output$chick_weight &lt;- renderPlotly({
    plot_ly(
      x = c(ag_data$time),
      y = c(ag_data$x),
      type = &quot;scatter&quot;,
      mode = &quot;lines&quot;,
      split = c(ag_data$diet),
      showlegend = FALSE
    ) |&gt;
      htmlwidgets::onRender(&quot;
        function(el, x) {
          var legendContainer = document.getElementById('legend_container');

          el.data.forEach(function(trace, i) {
            var color = trace.line ? trace.line.color : '#888';
            var label = trace.name;
            var visible = true;
            var btn = document.createElement('button');
            btn.className = 'legend-btn';
            btn.style.borderColor = color;
            var text = document.createElement('span');
            text.innerText = label;
            btn.appendChild(text);

            btn.onclick = function() {
              visible = !visible;
              Plotly.restyle(el, { visible: visible }, [i]);
              btn.classList.toggle('dimmed', !visible);
            };

            legendContainer.appendChild(btn);
          });
        }
      &quot;)
  })
}

shinyApp(ui = ui, server = server)
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/jtb3lhSF.gif" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/jtb3lhSF.gif" alt="res" /></a></p>

Q: How can I apply CSS to only the first div when both divs have the same class name? A: <p>A proper bullet-proof version (to target a <em><strong>first-of-class</strong></em>) would be to use the a more specific (yet complicated) selector like <code>.circle:not(.circle ~ .circle)</code></p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>/* select first-of-class, really */
.circle:not(.circle~.circle) {
  background: red;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div class="parent"&gt;
  &lt;b&gt;Bold&lt;/b&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;DIV no class&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="circle"&gt;DIV.circle&lt;/div&gt; &lt;!-- THIS ONE! --&gt;
  &lt;b&gt;Bold&lt;/b&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;DIV no class&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="circle"&gt;DIV.circle&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="circle"&gt;DIV.circle&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>which will take care to <strong>really</strong> target the first occurrence of the desired <code>.circle</code> element, even if preceded by other tags.</p>
<hr />
<p>PS: also, make sure to fix your typos in CSS <code>width=&quot;100&quot;; height=&quot;200&quot;;</code> should be <code>width: 100px; height: 200px;</code></p>
